WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.890
- Speaking of true love
though, at Christmas,

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:05.160
my next guests have written
the perfect holiday love story.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.500
It's called "Whiteout,"
and it's a collaboration

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.470
between six of the most
successful Black female authors

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:11.667
in young adult fiction

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:14.793
(audience claps and cheers)
Two of them are with us now.

00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:17.817
Please welcome Angie Thomas
and Nicola Yoon, everybody.

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.363
(all clapping)
- Thank you.

00:00:20.363 --> 00:00:21.196
- So, meet Poppy.

00:00:21.196 --> 00:00:22.154
- Hi.

00:00:22.154 --> 00:00:23.266
- Hi, nice to meet you.
- Meet Linda.

00:00:23.266 --> 00:00:24.239
- [Poppy] Nice to meet you.

00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:25.380
- Nice to meet you.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.480
- With six authors, who writes what?

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.860
How do you choose that?

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.018
- Well, Dhonielle is our
Tony Stark brain operation.

00:00:32.018 --> 00:00:33.930
(all laughing)

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.860
Yeah, she likes to say
she has executive skills.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:38.957
We'll call it that.

00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:40.483
(all laughing)

00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:41.316
- You say she's bossy.
- Bossy.

00:00:41.316 --> 00:00:42.990
- A little bit bossy.
- Yeah, she's bossy.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:47.990
But with "Blackout," she saw
that there were all these,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.650
there was a lack of stories

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.330
about Black kids falling in love,

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.590
and her niece wanted that.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.707
And so she said, you know,

00:00:53.707 --> 00:00:56.520
"Why don't we all take
a trope and write it?"

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.780
Because these are, you know,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.660
like best friends falling
in love, enemies to lovers,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.493
these sort of things.

00:01:01.493 --> 00:01:03.420
We don't get to see Black
kids doing that a lot.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.257
And so she assigned us
tropes, and she was like,

00:01:05.257 --> 00:01:06.780
"All right, this is
what I want you to do."

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.700
And so we did the same
thing with "Whiteout,"

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.460
and it was just so much
fun getting to, you know,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.380
write these stories about
these different kids,

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.670
and they're all connected.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.440
So it's not, we call it a novel

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.640
as opposed to an anthology.
- Right, yeah.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.070
With chapters instead
of like short stories

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.690
because they're all connected,

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.940
and they're all leading to one big goal.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.250
- Oh, this reminds me
of like "Love Actually."

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.120
Yeah, how they all,

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.100
and this is like one of
my favorite films ever.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.590
And it, I just, I love when films do that

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.290
or stories do that, it somehow
all ties together at the end.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.250
That's so clever.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.050
- Right, and it's like
kind of a little mystery,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.580
you don't know how it's
gonna fit together.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.730
and it's such a nice payoff
when you see it come together.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.612
And it's a big, like there's
a lot of kissing in this book.

00:01:44.612 --> 00:01:45.445
It's like

00:01:45.445 --> 00:01:46.442
a big romantic love story.
- Nothing wrong with that,

00:01:46.442 --> 00:01:47.717
Nicola.
(all laughing)

00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:48.930
Nothing wrong with a smooch.
- The more kissing,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.910
the better, right?
- Yeah.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.310
So both of you write with
teen readers in mind.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.560
So, what stories inspire you?

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.910
Is it like maybe kids you know?

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.410
Like maybe, she said her niece, right,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.180
is how this all kind of happened right?

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.170
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.600
I'm inspired just by
young people in general.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.010
They are so amazing.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.930
We have to give this generation credit.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.990
And, you know, and there's
so many types of stories

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:10.920
you can tell about them.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.290
So many may have so many
different experiences,

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.020
and I think as adults we would
be doing them a disservice

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.700
if we only told one kind of story.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.160
And so it's just, it's respecting them,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.690
respecting what they love

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.850
respecting that they
fall in love, you know,

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.520
respecting that they,
you know, have arguments,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.530
and that they have their
drama, whatever it may be.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.450
Respecting that and
saying, "You know what?

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.010
I'm gonna give you a mirror. Here you go.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:32.850
You're awesome.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.440
Hopefully, I show you how awesome you are

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.670
a little bit more."
- Yeah.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.018
And you have the opportunity

00:02:38.018 --> 00:02:38.970
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.280
to represent so many
different kinds of people.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.740
'Cause all the stories, you
know, they come together,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.420
but, you know, just
like in "Love Actually,"

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.520
there's so many different,
you know, lives going on,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.630
so many different walks of life.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:49.470
That's kind of cool too.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.303
- Well, I mean,

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:53.040
what happens I think when you
see stories with Black kids,

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.140
you see the same kind of story, right?

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.430
But Black kids fall in love.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.070
Like I fell in love every day as a child,

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.698
like a teenager, right?
- Yeah, you are my daughter.

00:03:00.698 --> 00:03:02.190
(Nicola laughs)

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.260
- Right, so we fall in
love, we're vulnerable,

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.900
we like kissing, we fall
in love with ourselves.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.180
We deserve forgiveness and second chances.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.140
You know, we deserve the big
spoony aspirational love story,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.370
and we don't see enough of those.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.587
So this is our love letter
to those kids to say,

00:03:17.587 --> 00:03:21.000
"Hey, we know you fall in
love. Like we did too."

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.320
- They wanna see themselves though

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.730
in that story.
- Yeah, they do.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.140
- I agree. I think that's so cool.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.000
Backstage we have a teenage writer

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.050
who says Angie and Nicola
are her two favorite authors.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.880
She thinks she's here to
talk about her book club,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.640
which we shall cover, but she has no idea

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.260
they read one of her short stories

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.520
and have plenty of feedback.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.280
So, we're going to ask
Angie and Nicola to hide

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.969
during the break, behind
a Christmas tree perhaps.

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:44.370
(Angie and Nicola laugh)
But stick around.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.553
You don't wanna miss this
surprise. It's gonna be so cool.

00:03:46.553 --> 00:03:48.060
(audience clapping)
So, my next guest

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.680
is a 10th grader and member

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.680
of the Breedlove Readers Book
Club in Syracuse, New York.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.140
It's a book club specifically
for young Black girls

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.780
featuring a diverse range of authors.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.280
She's also an aspiring writer

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.160
'cause I was not this cool
as a sophomore, but whatever.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.210
Please welcome, Gabrielle, everybody.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.205
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:04:06.205 --> 00:04:07.752
- [Kelly] Meet Linda, meet Poppy.

00:04:07.752 --> 00:04:08.585
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:04:08.585 --> 00:04:09.418
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.540
- So, it's so cool,
you're like in 10th grade,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:13.770
you're part of this cool book club.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.540
So, tell me like what do y'all read?

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.070
What is the book club like?

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.380
- The book club is a Black girl book club

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.590
for girls in central New
York where we come together,

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.420
and we just share our experiences.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.280
We read, we write, we incorporate
art projects into it all.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.780
And I knew it was perfect for me

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.130
because my dad is a retired
pro basketball player,

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.110
and we traveled all across Europe.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.590
We lived in Russia, Greece,
Italy, Croatia, and France.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:41.580
- [Kelly] What a childhood!

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.130
- Yes, yes it was.
(chuckles) It was very fun.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.070
And during that time I didn't
really have a constant,

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.620
so reading was that constant for me.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.520
- That's so cool.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.770
So, what are some of the
books that y'all have read?

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:57.330
- We have read "American
Street" by Ibi Zoboi.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:02.010
We have read "Grown"
by Tiffany D. Jackson.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.620
And my personal favorite is "Blackout,"

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.660
which is written by six different authors.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.943
Two of them happen to
be my favorite authors.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.100
Angie Thomas, actually, she
wrote "The Hate U Give."

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:14.933
- I've read that, yeah.

00:05:14.933 --> 00:05:17.790
- And the main character,
as you know, Star,

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.590
she goes to a predominantly white school,

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.810
and I happen to go to
predominantly white school too.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.210
So, just seeing a girl who has-

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.410
- [Kelly] You saw
yourself in the character.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.600
- Exactly. I saw myself in this character.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.220
- That's incredible. Wow.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.590
So, well, I have two people backstage

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:33.690
who have actually read
Gabrielle's short story.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.070
'Cause you wrote a short story right?

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.890
- Yes, called the "Deadly Scavenger Hunt."

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.110
It's about a girl who has to compete

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.180
to get a scholarship for college.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.020
- Okay, so, well,

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.450
both are number one New York
Times bestselling authors

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.120
and four of their books have
been turned into movies,

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.160
so they're pretty good at what they do.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.206
They're what we call
a "Whole Lotta Woman."

00:05:52.206 --> 00:05:54.789
(upbeat music)

00:05:56.406 --> 00:05:57.239
(audience clapping)

00:05:57.239 --> 00:06:00.127
So, Angie Thomas and Nicola
Yoon, come on out! (laughs)

00:06:00.127 --> 00:06:03.367
(upbeat music)
(audience claps and cheers)

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:07.700
(Angie and Nicola laughing)
- What?!

00:06:11.206 --> 00:06:12.657
- [Angie] It is so nice to meet you.

00:06:12.657 --> 00:06:13.870
- It is so nice to meet you too.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.270
- Oh my God.
- Hi.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.136
- [Nicola] It's so nice to meet you.

00:06:17.136 --> 00:06:18.429
- Oh my God.

00:06:18.429 --> 00:06:19.663
(audience clapping)

00:06:19.663 --> 00:06:21.220
That was perfect.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.812
You like ended with black, and I was like,

00:06:22.812 --> 00:06:26.157
"All right, segue."
(Angie laughing)

00:06:26.157 --> 00:06:27.780
Have a seat. Oh my gosh.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.843
So, both of you Red
Gabrielle's short story.

00:06:30.843 --> 00:06:32.190
- Yes.
- So, any feedback,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.060
what are you thinking?

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.080
- Oh my God.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.450
That twist, that cliffhanger, like-

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:37.590
- Oh my God, you got us.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:38.610
- Like, yeah, you got us.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:39.630
Like, oh my goodness,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.330
I could not write
anything like that at 15.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.490
Your dialogue is amazing.

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.680
Your character development,
your twist, all of that.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:47.880
Like you got it.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:48.960
- Yeah, you really do.
- Thank you.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:49.830
- The suspense.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:52.080
Like I flew through that thing,

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.340
and at the end I was like hmm!

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:54.173
- [Angie] We won't spoil it.

00:06:54.173 --> 00:06:55.724
- I was like, "Don't you spoil alert!"

00:06:55.724 --> 00:06:57.057
- No, I will not.

00:06:57.057 --> 00:07:00.450
- I wanna read it, so I
like a twist at the end.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:03.120
So, what do you wanna say to these authors

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.890
that have inspired you?

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.880
- Just thank you for your
contribution to the world

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.390
and just showing Black
girls that we can do it too.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:13.568
- Yes, we can.
- Absolutely!

00:07:13.568 --> 00:07:14.863
(audience clapping)

00:07:14.863 --> 00:07:16.560
I know you write, Poppy,

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.230
like what would you give
advice like on, you know,

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.330
just being a young writer, what
do you think is good advice?

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.430
- I mean, in the presence
of extraordinary writers,

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:25.920
I'm like "Ah!"
(all laughing)

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.770
Your life sounds like it's
been so fascinating already,

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.770
and, you know, you've got so much to tell.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:36.090
You know, our movie is about my real life,

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.820
and I sort of tend to stick to what I know

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.100
and not be afraid to
tell the truth about it

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.500
'cause there's some
skeletons in the closet

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.020
that'll be in the sequel
to the first movie.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.180
But, yeah, just not to be ashamed

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.970
of the things you think
people would judge,

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:51.900
that's sort of the stuff

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.000
that you tend to wanna throw out there.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.080
- It's more relatable as well.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:57.660
- Yeah, yeah.
- Well, everybody,

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.850
remember to pick up a copy of "Whiteout"

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.200
wherever books are sold.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:02.730
But for everyone in our audience,

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:03.791
you're so lucky you
don't have to do a thing

00:08:03.791 --> 00:08:07.021
because you're all going home
with a copy of "Whiteout!"

00:08:07.021 --> 00:08:09.113
(audience clapping and cheering)

